{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/getting-started.md at exact clean pushed head be496c6d293cddec78dc64e8d0ccb0cc8eb3aa7a on request/getting-started-runnable, based on main 9ec7109c, fulfilling planner's 23310281 under promise d5cf4482. It closes the four content defects planner found by walking the page from an empty directory, recorded in assert fc4b4ea7 and checked again against the previous head in c2a89ac3.\n\nGITSEQ IS NOW ASSIGNED WHERE THE READER STANDS. The page named $GITSEQ in prose and never set it. Unset, claude mcp add succeeds and registers /bin/gitseq-mcp, and the reader learns of it several steps later as a session that will not connect. It is exported alongside PATH in the checkout, before any cd, and the claude-code section points back at that export rather than assuming it.\n\nTHE READINESS LOOP NOW PROVES THIS RESIDENT ANSWERED FOR THIS WORKROOM. The old loop discarded stderr, and gs status pointed at a resident serving a different workroom exits 0 after falling back to a local read. PORT defaults to 7777, which is taken on any machine already running a resident, so a failed bind read as success and the browser showed somebody else's workroom. The loop now checks the serve process is alive and treats the fallback notice as not-ready, and a timeout or dead server ends in a loud exit 1. I REPRODUCED THE NEGATIVE CASE MYSELF rather than accept it: a resident for one workroom on port 7921, then the page's block extracted verbatim from this committed file and run for a second workroom on the same port, gives 'gs: listen tcp 127.0.0.1:7921: bind: address already in use' followed by 'no resident for this workroom on port 7921' and exit 1. The live resident on 7777 was never touched. The default port stays 7777 because it is gs serve's own documented default and appears on ten other pages; what changed is that a taken port now fails loudly, and the prose says to set PORT if 7777 is busy.\n\nTHE SHELL LIFETIME IS STATED. The trap ties the resident to this shell, and the page now says to keep the terminal open, what silently degrades if it closes, and where to look to run a resident that outlives a shell.\n\nMAKE TEST CARRIES A DURATION WARNING. About ten minutes with little visible progress, dominated by the documentation gates, which a first-time reader would otherwise read as a hang.\n\nTwo smaller points from the same walk are applied: the loop prompt names the MCP tools, because a weaker model told only to check for work items loaded the skill and never called them; and the roster sample is the pretty-printed shape gs actors really emits.\n\nCITATIONS. All four bases are merge-published artifacts on current main, three of them ordinarily stale, following planner's ruling in 0e332b15: a candidate basis is a basis whose head might die, and world staleness inherited from a dead candidate is refused by gs merge where ordinary staleness is merely recorded.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THIS EXACT HEAD: go test -count=1 ./internal/docset green, 112.8s, no cached result -- and that gate executes the page's sh blocks itself, so the new readiness loop passed under the real check with a gate-supplied port. git diff --check clean, worktree clean, origin equals the head. No Go files touched.","body":{"commit":"be496c6d293cddec78dc64e8d0ccb0cc8eb3aa7a","path":"docs/getting-started.md"}}